From fc255426b7d9484a43cce837ef16cddfa1a947fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cynthia Krafft <cynthiacondra@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 15:10:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] stats: grow the coverage ring buffer instead of wrapping
 around

The coverage distribution (COV rows, and the -t/-g "percentage of target
genome with coverage" summary) is accumulated in a ring buffer of five times
the longest read length seen, indexed modulo its size relative to the
current read start.  An aligned block that ends further than that beyond
the read start, such as the far exon of a spliced RNA-seq alignment, wrapped
around and was added to positions near the read start.  With 100 bp reads
the buffer holds 1,500 positions, so any intron longer than about 1.4 kb
triggered it; on a simulated six-exon gene the covered-position count came
out 18% too high and "percentage of target genome with coverage > 0" was
reported as 117.77%.

round_buffer_insert_read() now grows the buffer (doubling) whenever a block
would reach beyond it, linearising the pending counts.  The same helper
replaces the copy in realloc_buffers(), which passed element counts to
memcpy() where byte counts were needed and so dropped three quarters of the
pending counts whenever a read longer than every read seen so far arrived
while coverage was pending near the end of the ring (mixed-length or
long-read data).

Two regression tests cover the two cases; both fail on the previous code.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
